
  Two rules beyond the name check, both aimed at the ways an allowlist usually leaks:

  - [1mAllowlisted keys must hold primitives.[0m A string, number, boolean, or null passes; anything else is replaced with
    [38;5;153m"[object]"[39m / [38;5;153m"[array]"[39m / [38;5;153m"[function]"[39m. Without this, an allowlisted [38;5;153mreason[39m field holding an error object with the
    request body attached would carry a password straight through a filter that "passed."
  - [1mStrings truncate at 200 characters[0m, with an ellipsis. Cheap guard against a stack trace or a serialized payload
    becoming a log line.

  Rejected keys are not silent. The record carries [38;5;153m"_dropped":["username","password"][39m listing the rejected key [3mnames[0m
  only — names aren't the secret, values are, and without this you get a debugging session that ends in "why is my field
  missing?" rather than "right, it's not allowlisted."

  [1mLevel resolution.[0m [38;5;153mresolveLevel(candidates)[39m takes an ordered array of raw strings and returns the first valid one,
  falling back to [38;5;153minfo[39m. Browser passes [38;5;153m[?log= param, localStorage "logLevel"][39m; Node passes [38;5;153m[process.env.LOG_LEVEL][39m. The
  URL param wins for that page load and does not write to storage — a support link shouldn't silently leave someone in
  debug mode forever.

  An invalid value falls through to the next candidate and emits one [38;5;153mwarn[39m record, [38;5;153mlogger.bad_level[39m, carrying the [3msource[0m
  ([38;5;153m"url"[39m, [38;5;153m"storage"[39m, [38;5;153m"env"[39m) rather than the offending value. A typo shouldn't be silent, but the URL param is
  user-controlled input, and echoing user-controlled strings into a log that may later ship to a collector is how log
  injection starts.

  [1mThe logger never breaks the app.[0m Sink calls are wrapped; a throwing sink is swallowed and latched off after one
  [38;5;153mconsole.error[39m, so a broken transport degrades to no logs rather than to an error per log line, or to a login form that
  stops working. Per-key reads in the filter are guarded too, so a throwing getter on a passed object can't take down
  the caller.

  Node writes one JSON line per record — stdout for [38;5;153mdebug[39m/[38;5;153minfo[39m, stderr for [38;5;153mwarn[39m/[38;5;153merror[39m. The browser maps to
  [38;5;153mconsole.debug/info/warn/error[39m and passes the record as an object so devtools keeps it expandable.

  Look right? Section 3 covers the call sites, and there are two behavior changes in it I want your explicit sign-off
  on.

